\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Effect of the PET and rigidity losses on the test set (28 patients, 38 pairs). Each network is trained with the given losses and evaluated as is (left) and with instance optimisation (IO) using the same losses (right). Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than ``Neither'' in the same block (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$).}}{16}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablations}{{2}{16}{Effect of the PET and rigidity losses on the test set (28 patients, 38 pairs). Each network is trained with the given losses and evaluated as is (left) and with instance optimisation (IO) using the same losses (right). Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than ``Neither'' in the same block (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$)}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Losses in training and IO versus only in IO (test set, 28 patients). For each loss set, filled: network trained with the losses, then IO with them; hollow: network trained without them (T1), same IO. Grey: neither loss (I1). Points: patient means with 95\,\% patient-bootstrap confidence intervals.}}{16}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:q2}{{2}{16}{Losses in training and IO versus only in IO (test set, 28 patients). For each loss set, filled: network trained with the losses, then IO with them; hollow: network trained without them (T1), same IO. Grey: neither loss (I1). Points: patient means with 95\,\% patient-bootstrap confidence intervals}{figure.2}{}}
