\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Per-lesion volume change on the test set (1327 lesions of 26 patients), by location (soft tissue / bone-associated, $\geq $50\,\% of the lesion on bone) and size (small $<$0.5\,ml, medium $<$5\,ml, large). Median $|\Delta \mathrm  {MTV}|$ per lesion; left: network output, right: with instance optimisation (IO) using the same losses. Error bars: 95\,\% confidence intervals from a patient-cluster bootstrap. Lesions below 5 voxels are excluded.}}{18}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lesions}{{3}{18}{Per-lesion volume change on the test set (1327 lesions of 26 patients), by location (soft tissue / bone-associated, $\geq $50\,\% of the lesion on bone) and size (small $<$0.5\,ml, medium $<$5\,ml, large). Median $|\Delta \mathrm {MTV}|$ per lesion; left: network output, right: with instance optimisation (IO) using the same losses. Error bars: 95\,\% confidence intervals from a patient-cluster bootstrap. Lesions below 5 voxels are excluded}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods.}}{19}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{3}{19}{Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods}{table.3}{}}
